WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:10.080
On the 25th of July 306 AD, Constantius I, ruler&nbsp;
of the western provinces of the Roman Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.560
died at York in northern England. The legions&nbsp;
there immediately proclaimed his son as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:24.960
new emperor, Constantine I. Little could&nbsp;
they have known that summer day in 306 AD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.520
that the man to whom they had just sworn their&nbsp;
allegiance would change the course of history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:40.160
How did Constantine go from York in 306 AD to&nbsp;
converting the Roman Empire to Christianity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:47.280
and building one of the greatest cities in the&nbsp;
world? This is the story of Constantine the Great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:01:04.640
the ruler who converted the Roman&nbsp;
Empire to Christianity.  
&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:11.920
The man known to history as Emperor Constantine&nbsp;
I or Constantine the Great is believed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:20.160
been born on the 27th of February in the year&nbsp;
272 AD. While the day on which he was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:27.200
the 27th of February, is known with near certainty&nbsp;
from ancient sources, there is more of a debate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:36.640
about the year. He could have been born in&nbsp;
273 AD or 274 AD, although some historians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:46.400
place his year of birth as late as 280 AD. The&nbsp;
strongest evidence supports the year 272 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:55.600
as several fourth-century writers claimed he was&nbsp;
65 years old when he died in May 337 AD. His full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:03.120
Roman name was Flavius Constantinus, to which was&nbsp;
later added the name Valerius as well. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.280
the year of his birth is disputed, there is&nbsp;
agreement that he was born in the city of Naissus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:17.360
in the Roman province of Moesia Superior. This&nbsp;
is the modern-day city of Niš in Serbia.  
&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:25.360
Constantine’s father was Flavius Constantius,&nbsp;
a Roman military commander who was born in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.360
Balkans. around Moesia, where Serbia&nbsp;
and Kosovo lie today. He was, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.880
said to be either an Illyrian or Thracian,&nbsp;
the tribal groups who in ancient times had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:44.400
dominated the regions around modern-day Croatia,&nbsp;
Albania and Bulgaria. When Constantine was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.240
Constantius was a young soldier. He would&nbsp;
rise to a position of major power within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.800
the Roman Empire as Constantine was growing&nbsp;
up. Constantius entered into a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:06.240
with a Greek woman named Helena around 270 AD.&nbsp;
Constantine was their first and only child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.080
Helena came from a humble background. She was&nbsp;
an important figure in the history of the era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:18.320
for while Constantius was an adherent of the old&nbsp;
polytheistic religion of Rome and Greece, Helena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:26.000
was a Christian. As such, she seemingly exposed&nbsp;
her son to the Christian faith at an early age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.200
It is unclear if Constantius and Helena were in a&nbsp;
legally binding union of any kind. He later left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:42.080
her and married a woman named Theodora with whom&nbsp;
he had numerous further children. 
	
&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:48.720
Constantine’s life is unusually well documented&nbsp;
for a later Roman Emperor. While the emperors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.960
of the first century AD were written about&nbsp;
extensively by ancient historians like Suetonius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:02.640
Tacitus and Cassius Dio, there are far fewer&nbsp;
biographies for later emperors. That changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.480
completely for Constantine. His role in&nbsp;
converting the Roman Empire to Christianity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.480
meant that dozens of Christian historians in&nbsp;
the fourth and fifth centuries wrote about him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.280
Several of these accounts were written by&nbsp;
contemporaries of Constantine who lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.680
through the events of his reign. The most&nbsp;
notable is the Vita Constantini or Life of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:32.400
Constantine written by Eusebius, the greatest&nbsp;
early historian of the Christian church. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:40.160
was born a few years before Constantine and died&nbsp;
two years after him, so their lives overlapped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:46.720
Subsequent Christian historians, such as&nbsp;
Sozomen added further details. Lactantius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.080
a slightly earlier Christian historian, knew&nbsp;
Constantine in the first half of his reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.520
and wrote a work entitled On the Deaths of&nbsp;
the Persecutors which shed a lot of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.880
on Constantine’s earlier years. However, while&nbsp;
Constantine’s life and reign are unusually well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:11.280
documented, the nature of these sources creates&nbsp;
a problem in and of itself. They were nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.720
all written by Christian scholars who wrote&nbsp;
about Constantine in hagiographical terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.560
highlighting his successes in converting the&nbsp;
empire to Christianity. As such, they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.320
clearly biased accounts that exaggerated&nbsp;
Constantine’s accomplishments.  
&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:37.200
When Constantine was born in the early 270s AD the&nbsp;
Roman Empire had been in the midst of a crisis for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:46.240
nearly forty years. The Severan Dynasty which&nbsp;
had ruled the empire between 193 AD and 235 AD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:55.680
had been succeeded by decades of civil war. There&nbsp;
were dozens of emperors between 235 AD and 284 AD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.280
On top of this, the northern and eastern borders&nbsp;
of the empire were assailed by a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.240
Germanic tribes. These included the Franks and&nbsp;
Sarmatians in Europe and the Sassanid Persians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.640
Palmyrans in the Middle East. Alongside these&nbsp;
assaults from outsiders, hyperinflation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:21.440
pandemics impacted on the empire economically and&nbsp;
socially. The Third Century Crisis only came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:29.120
an end in 284 AD when the Emperor Diocletian rose&nbsp;
to the throne. Constantine’s father had decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:35.920
to support Diocletian’s claim to the throne. As&nbsp;
a result, Constantius benefited accordingly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:44.800
Diocletian’s 21-year long reign began. In 293 AD,&nbsp;
in order to ensure a peaceful transfer of power in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:51.440
the future, Diocletian established a college of&nbsp;
emperors known as the Tetrarchy. This structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:58.640
outlined a new format for Roman rule. There would&nbsp;
be two senior emperors, known as the augusti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:06.240
and two junior emperors, known as the caesares.&nbsp;
He appointed Constantius as one of the caesares.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.080
As such, while Constantine was growing up, his&nbsp;
father’s support for Diocletian promoted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:18.560
family to become part of the ruling elite. If&nbsp;
all went well, the idea was that Constantius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:24.720
would subsequently succeed to become one&nbsp;
of the more senior augusti. Accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:31.680
this could open a place for Constantine to&nbsp;
enter the Tetrarchy at that point. 
	
&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.640
These shifting political events meant that&nbsp;
Constantine did not spend much time around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.160
his father when he was young. When he was an&nbsp;
infant, for instance, Constantius was serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:53.280
as a commander of the personal guard of Aurelian,&nbsp;
the Emperor between 270 AD and 275 AD. He moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.480
around constantly in the years that followed and&nbsp;
it is likely that Constantine was primarily raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:05.680
by his mother, Helena. Eusebius stated in his&nbsp;
Life of Constantine that the future emperor had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.520
“reaped the advantages of a liberal education.”&nbsp;
This involved learning subjects like history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:18.240
philosophy and rhetoric that the Romans prized&nbsp;
greatly. He was taught in Latin and this became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:24.000
his primary language despite his mother’s&nbsp;
Greek heritage. Later in life Constantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:30.320
required translators to aid him in some official&nbsp;
duties in the Greek-speaking east. Constantine’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.320
teenage years were spent at Diocletian’s court&nbsp;
in the city of Nicomedia, modern-day Ízmit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:43.040
western Turkey. Historians remain divided on&nbsp;
how directly exposed he was to Christianity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:50.000
here during his youth. For instance, one of the&nbsp;
learned preachers in Nicomedia during this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.880
of Constantine’s childhood was the Christian&nbsp;
scholar Lactantius. As we have seen, Lactantius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:06.400
later became a key source on events in the Roman&nbsp;
Empire during Constantine’s rise to power. 
&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:12.960
Diocletian was certainly not a supporter of&nbsp;
Christians of any kind. Christianity had grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.080
massively in the third century to become&nbsp;
part of the politics of the Roman Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.200
The new religion had started out as a radical&nbsp;
sect of Judaism in the Levant in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:29.440
of the first century AD. It spread thereafter&nbsp;
through the Greek-speaking Eastern Mediterranean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:36.320
yet it remained a minor faith with small, isolated&nbsp;
communities of Christians in places like Antioch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.960
Corinth, Alexandria and the coastal cities of&nbsp;
what is now Turkey. It continued to grow slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:49.840
albeit steadily in the second century AD. From&nbsp;
the 160s AD onwards there were a growing number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:55.200
of Pagan Greco-Roman writers in the Eastern&nbsp;
Mediterranean, pointing out that the values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:02.000
of the Christians fundamentally conflicted with&nbsp;
those of mainstream Roman society. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.680
despite the claims made by later Christian&nbsp;
writers, there was no sustained policy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:15.120
persecuting Christians in the Roman Empire until&nbsp;
the reign of the Emperor Decius between 249 AD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:22.320
and 251 AD. The shift in attitude was occasioned&nbsp;
by the massive growth of the Christian movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:30.240
in the third century. Historians, theologians and&nbsp;
even sociologists still debate why this occurred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:35.760
The most likely explanation is that the Third&nbsp;
Century Crisis created such a volatile political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:42.080
and social environment across the Roman Empire&nbsp;
that this radical religion, with its emphasis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.880
salvation in the afterlife, became attractive to&nbsp;
a growing number of people. Even Decius’ campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.960
of persecution was brief. Instead, the most&nbsp;
substantial attack on the Christians came during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.880
the later years of the reign of Diocletian, by&nbsp;
which time there were several million Christians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:09.360
across the empire. In 303 AD he launched the Great&nbsp;
Persecution. In this the rights of Christians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.800
across the empire were restricted and they were&nbsp;
ordered to make the standard sacrifices to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:22.000
emperor on pain of punishment. As a member of the&nbsp;
Tetrarchy, Constantine’s father was responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:30.920
for overseeing the Great Persecution in some&nbsp;
of the western provinces of the empire. 
&nbsp;

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.000
Could you use “DESS-eee-us here
	
Diocletian’s initiation of the Great Persecution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:42.000
was one of the last major acts of his eventful&nbsp;
reign. Two years later, on the 1st of May 305 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:48.560
he made the unusual decision to retire and&nbsp;
hand over the office of augustus. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.280
all seemingly in the interest of ensuring&nbsp;
that the Tetrarchy system functioned as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:59.680
was supposed to and there was a smooth transfer&nbsp;
of power. In the western provinces, Constantius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:06.560
was elevated to become one of the augusti. In the&nbsp;
east, Constantine had hoped that when Diocletian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:13.120
stepped down that he might be appointed as one of&nbsp;
the caesares, the junior emperors in the Tetrarchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:20.640
system. However, this did not happen. Instead,&nbsp;
the new senior emperor in the east, Galerius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:27.440
appointed his nephew, Maximinus Daza, as the&nbsp;
new caesar in the east. Fearing that he might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:33.360
now be killed by Galerius, who was effectively&nbsp;
holding him hostage at his court, Constantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:40.240
fled westwards and joined his father in Gaul near&nbsp;
modern-day Boulogne in northern France. When he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:46.080
reached Gaul, his father was preparing to lead&nbsp;
an army over the English Channel to campaign in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:52.480
Britain. This was against the Picts of Caledonia&nbsp;
who were pressing south from what is now Scotland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:59.920
into northern England. Constantius was seemingly&nbsp;
already ill and he died at Eboracum, the name of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:07.360
the Roman city of York, on the 25th of July 306&nbsp;
AD. The legions there immediately proclaimed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:13.840
son as his successor, a move which was a breach&nbsp;
of the Tetrarchy system, as Constantine had never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:20.080
been established as one of the caesares. Hence,&nbsp;
civil war beckoned yet again within months of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:27.360
Diocletian’s abdication. For his part, Constantine&nbsp;
later made it part of his official propaganda that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:34.320
he had not requested the legions to proclaim him&nbsp;
as emperor at York. Constantine argued that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:40.880
had done so anyway, in the process creating the&nbsp;
notion that he was a figure of such popularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:47.280
that he had had the imperial title thrust upon&nbsp;
him. The Civil Wars of the Tetrarchy that now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:57.360
began in 306 AD, and which would last down to&nbsp;
324 AD, would change the course of history. 
&nbsp;

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.800
MAX-imin-us
If the Tetrarchy system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:06.800
had functioned in the way that it was supposed&nbsp;
to, a commander named Severus, who had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:13.680
the caesar in the western provinces in 305 AD&nbsp;
when Constantius became one of the augusti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:20.320
should have become the undisputed augusti in the&nbsp;
west when Constantius died. Constantine could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:26.240
then have been promoted to become the new caesar&nbsp;
if Severus had agreed that he should enter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:32.960
imperial college. Instead, though, Constantine&nbsp;
claimed his father’s senior position, opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:39.920
up a civil war between himself and Severus. The&nbsp;
picture was further complicated by Maxentius. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:48.720
was a son of Maximian, who had ruled alongside&nbsp;
Diocletian down to 305 AD. Like Constantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:56.000
Maxentius had been passed over for earlier&nbsp;
promotion. In October 306 AD, when news arrived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.320
to Italy where Maxentius was based at the time of&nbsp;
Constantius’ death, the legions under his control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:09.600
proclaimed him as the new emperor. Severus was&nbsp;
not far away in Milan and headed south to confront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:17.920
Maxentius. He was defeated, captured and died&nbsp;
not long afterwards in 307 AD. This meant that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:25.040
the struggle for power in the west of the empire&nbsp;
was now broadly between Constantine and Maxentius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:32.000
While Maxentius had control over Italy and&nbsp;
secured the provinces of North Africa, Constantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:42.080
secured control over Gaul and Hispania. 
Constantine and Maxentius had something in common;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:47.520
neither of them were able to gain acceptance as&nbsp;
a legitimate ruler in the west from the senior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:55.200
augustus in the east, Emperor Galerius. Instead,&nbsp;
at an imperial conference held at Carnuntum on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:03.600
11th of November 308 AD in what is now Austria,&nbsp;
it was decided that Licinius, an ally of Galerius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:10.400
would become the new augustus of the western&nbsp;
half of the empire. The conference at Carnuntum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:16.400
was given added gravitas by the presence of the&nbsp;
retired Emperor Diocletian. Although an effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:22.720
was made to conciliate Constantine by making&nbsp;
him caesar in the west, he was unsatisfied with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:30.080
this. Instead he briefly drew closer to Maxentius.&nbsp;
Constantine had entered into a marriage alliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:37.040
with his rival in the west the previous year by&nbsp;
marrying Maxentius’ sister, Fausta. Their father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:45.280
Maximian, who had abdicated his position as&nbsp;
augustus in 305 AD at the same time as Diocletian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:53.040
but who had re-entered the political fray in 306&nbsp;
AD, was also involved in these complex political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.040 --> 00:17:00.240
manoeuvres. Fausta was probably Constantine’s&nbsp;
second wife. Years earlier he had entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.960
into a relationship with a woman named Minervina&nbsp;
while he was residing at the court of Diocletian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:13.360
They had a son together named Crispus. It is&nbsp;
unclear if Minervina was Constantine’s wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:21.440
or a concubine. In any event, he put her aside&nbsp;
in 307 AD in order to marry Fausta and form an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:27.840
alliance with Maxentius. Constantine and Fausta&nbsp;
would remain married for many years and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:36.160
had five children together, three sons and two&nbsp;
daughters, Constantina, Constantine, Constantius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:42.640
Constans and Helena. However, the alliance&nbsp;
between Maxentius and Constantine disintegrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:50.880
long before most of these children were born. In&nbsp;
310 AD, Maxentius and Fausta’s father, Maximian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:57.360
tried to overthrow Constantine in Gaul. He was&nbsp;
defeated, and his actions led to a spiral of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:03.440
events which led to Constantine and Maxentius&nbsp;
entering into direct war with one another for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:10.240
control over the western provinces. By then&nbsp;
Maxentius had become extremely unpopular and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:18.000
facing local rebellions in Italy. And so it was&nbsp;
that Constantine campaigned towards Rome in 312&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:25.920
AD. The resulting clash between his and Maxentius’&nbsp;
forces at the Milvian Bridge outside Rome on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:36.320
28th of October 312 AD has become one of the&nbsp;
most famous military clashes in history. 
&nbsp;

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:42.000
On the surface, the story of the Battle&nbsp;
of the Milvian Bridge was quite routine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:47.760
Two rival claimants for the Roman imperial&nbsp;
title clashed in battle. Constantine’s forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:52.960
won the battle after Maxentius drowned in&nbsp;
the River Tiber, and Constantine became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:58.960
the unrivalled ruler of the western provinces.&nbsp;
However, the significance of the Battle of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:05.440
Milvian Bridge lies in Constantine’s alleged&nbsp;
conversion to Christianity as a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:11.840
the battle. There are actually two completely&nbsp;
different versions of what happened. Lactantius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:18.000
claimed in On the Deaths of the Persecutors&nbsp;
that Constantine had a dream vision the night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:25.200
before the battle in which he was instructed to&nbsp;
have his men adorn their shields with a Chi-Rho,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:31.520
an early Christian symbol of Christ. As a&nbsp;
result of doing so, his men won the battle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:38.320
Constantine was inspired thereafter to become a&nbsp;
Christian ruler. The other version of the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:45.520
Constantine’s conversion was related by Eusebius.&nbsp;
He claimed that during the battle Constantine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:53.760
his soldiers saw a shining Christian cross in the&nbsp;
sky. Constantine interpreted this as a divine sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.760 --> 00:20:00.480
of the providence of the Christian god. Eusebius’&nbsp;
version became more popular over time with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:07.280
epic appearance of a shining cross in the sky&nbsp;
as the battle raged around the emperor. 
&nbsp;

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:11.840
KAI-ROW
Constantine claimed that he was inspired enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:17.040
by his vision at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge&nbsp;
that he proceeded in the immediate aftermath of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:25.200
it to end the persecution of the Christians. In&nbsp;
February 313 AD, the Edict of Milan was issued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:32.160
that effect. Here it was proclaimed that “we have&nbsp;
given to those Christians free and unrestricted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:39.040
opportunity of religious worship.” Several things&nbsp;
are usually overlooked with the Edict of Milan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:46.400
however. Firstly, the Edict went on to declare&nbsp;
that “we have also conceded to other religions the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:52.960
right of open and free observance of their worship&nbsp;
for the sake of the peace of our times.” Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:59.840
the preamble of the Edict states very clearly&nbsp;
that it was being issued after Constantine had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:07.040
met with his major remaining rival for power. This&nbsp;
was the ruler of the eastern provinces, Licinius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:13.440
and at Milan both had agreed on the measure&nbsp;
together. Thus, the Edict of Milan was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:20.080
a declaration by Constantine that Christianity&nbsp;
was to be tolerated henceforth. It was a joint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:27.120
declaration by Constantine and Licinius that all&nbsp;
religions were to be tolerated across the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:32.720
in order to end religious tensions among the&nbsp;
Roman people. The question of what motivated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:38.400
Constantine has absorbed the attention of&nbsp;
historians for centuries. In all likelihood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:45.040
he and Licinius had decided that it was pointless&nbsp;
to try to curb the seemingly unstoppable rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:51.600
of Christianity. They decided to tolerate the&nbsp;
Christians as a means of stabilising the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:57.520
after the crises of the third century. Indeed,&nbsp;
one of the last acts of Galerius, the senior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:06.320
emperor in the east, prior to his death in 311&nbsp;
AD, had been to issue the Edict of Serdica. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:13.280
proclamation ended Diocletian’s Great Persecution&nbsp;
in the eastern provinces and established the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:23.280
idea of tolerance that Constantine and Licinius&nbsp;
guaranteed at Milan two years later. 
&nbsp;

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:29.280
The popular view is that Constantine firmly&nbsp;
committed to Christianity owing to his experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:35.440
at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge. According to&nbsp;
this version of events, by the later stages of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:41.920
reign the Roman Empire had become a Christian&nbsp;
state and steps were already being taken to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:48.160
eradicate Pagan superstition. That&nbsp;
at least is the myth of Constantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:53.840
who is venerated as a Christian saint. The&nbsp;
only problem is this is not what happened at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.840 --> 00:23:01.200
all. While Constantine did end the persecution&nbsp;
of the Christians through the Edict of Milan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:06.960
would move to establish a Christian church&nbsp;
throughout the empire as his reign went on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:13.840
he continued to worship the old gods and did not&nbsp;
view Christianity as being an exclusive religion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:21.680
For instance, long after the Milvian Bridge, coins&nbsp;
were issued with Constantine’s image on one side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:28.080
and images of Jupiter, the old Roman king of&nbsp;
the gods on the other. He also connected his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:36.240
reign to Sol Invictus, a Sun deity that had become&nbsp;
extremely popular in the third century AD. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:43.200
is evidence to indicate that Constantine viewed&nbsp;
the Christian god as being a Sun deity as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:48.800
Overall, his religious outlook was one&nbsp;
in which he viewed Christianity as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:55.440
new component of the broad tapestry of&nbsp;
religious life across the empire. He did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.440 --> 00:24:04.960
not reject the older religion or other cults&nbsp;
like that of Sol Invictus.  	
&nbsp;

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:12.160
The meeting between Constantine and Licinius&nbsp;
at Milan in 313 AD was important for multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:18.720
reasons. The Edict was issued there and a marriage&nbsp;
alliance was also entered into as Licinius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:25.120
married Constantine’s half-sister, Flavia Julia&nbsp;
Constantia. The alliance was a temporary one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:32.080
but Licinius needed it to secure his power. He&nbsp;
faced a rival claimant for power in the east,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:40.080
Maximinus Daza, the nephew of Galerius&nbsp;
who had been made a caesar back in 305 AD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:45.760
Indeed, Licinius might well have been the&nbsp;
driving force behind the Edict of Milan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:52.960
a greater extent than we realise. Maximinus Daza&nbsp;
was a persecutor of the Christians in the lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:59.280
he controlled in Egypt and the Levant, which were&nbsp;
provinces where Christianity had flourished since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:06.160
the second century AD. Licinius may have been&nbsp;
trying to undermine Daza’s position by appealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:13.280
to the Christians of those lands. Shortly after&nbsp;
the meeting at Milan, Licinius defeated Daza at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:21.360
the Battle of Tzirallum on the 30th of April 313&nbsp;
AD. He was killed soon after and Constantine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:28.640
Licinius briefly ruled the western and eastern&nbsp;
halves of the empire in relative peace. Renewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:36.800
conflict broke out between them, though, in&nbsp;
316 AD. Constantine won an initial victory at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:43.280
the Battle of Mardis that winter, but the tensions&nbsp;
between them continued to simmer and they returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:51.360
to war at the start of the 320s AD. Constantine’s&nbsp;
twin victories at sea and on land, at the Battles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:59.040
of the Hellespont and Chrysopolis respectively,&nbsp;
in the summer and autumn of 324 AD brought these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:05.440
civil wars to an end. This saw a conclusion to&nbsp;
the conflict that had followed the abdication of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:13.440
Diocletian and left Constantine as the sole ruler&nbsp;
of the Roman Empire, seventeen and a half years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:19.160
after he was first proclaimed as emperor. 
MAX-imin-us
&nbsp;

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:25.840
One of Constantine’s first acts as ruler of&nbsp;
a consolidated Roman Empire was to initiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:32.160
plans for a new capital. Nova Roma or&nbsp;
‘New Rome’, as it was initially named,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:38.720
was to be located where Europe met Asia. The new&nbsp;
capital would be strategically located to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:45.360
an emperor residing there to respond to attacks&nbsp;
against both the northern and eastern borders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:50.800
The location that he chose was on the European&nbsp;
side of the Bosporus, the waterway separating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:56.560
the Balkans from Turkey and the Mediterranean&nbsp;
Sea from the Black Sea. The symbolic foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.560 --> 00:27:03.840
of the city of Constantinople, as it eventually&nbsp;
became known, here on the 24th of November 326&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:10.560
AD was not a revolutionary break with the past.&nbsp;
There had been a settlement at this location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:15.520
known as Byzantium, going back centuries to&nbsp;
when the Greeks had colonised this part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:22.240
the world. Furthermore, Diocletian had recognised&nbsp;
the need for a capital in this part of the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:29.520
to respond to issues as they arose in the east.&nbsp;
He had therefore kept his court at Nicomedia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:35.120
where Constantine had spent a large&nbsp;
part of his youth and early adult years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:42.160
Constantinople was closely modelled after Rome in&nbsp;
many ways. For instance, a claim was made that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:48.080
new capital was being built on seven hills, like&nbsp;
Rome had been, while the city centre was laid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:55.600
out to mimic Rome. What made Constantinople as&nbsp;
distinctive was its Christian character. Christian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.600 --> 00:28:01.920
churches were located prominently here, albeit&nbsp;
the grand church of Hagia Sophia was not built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.600
until much later. 
 
&nbsp;

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:13.520
Eusebius’ extremely extensive Life of Constantine&nbsp;
contains limited details on the emperor’s early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:19.920
life. Conversely, it is immensely detailed about&nbsp;
Constantine’s actions after he secured full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:27.360
control of the empire in 324 AD. It is also highly&nbsp;
informative about the reforms that he initiated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:33.040
to establish a hierarchical Christian Church&nbsp;
that was interconnected with the Roman state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:40.320
Constantine did so at an unprecedented ecumenical&nbsp;
council which he convened at the city of Nicaea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:48.720
on the Turkish side of the Bosporus in the summer&nbsp;
of 325 AD. This was presided over by Constantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:55.360
and attended by around 250 bishops drawn from&nbsp;
across the empire, and even from the lands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.360 --> 00:29:01.680
the Scythians and Persians beyond Rome’s borders.&nbsp;
The Council of Nicaea was a defining event in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:07.600
early history of Christianity. The Council saw&nbsp;
the Christian church organised more effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:13.920
and adjudication over a number of theological&nbsp;
disputes. These included the dating of Easter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:18.320
the establishment of what became known&nbsp;
as the Nicene Creed as a statement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:26.000
Christian faith and, most importantly of all, the&nbsp;
deliberations on the nature of the Holy Trinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:31.520
The issue of the Trinity had become controversial&nbsp;
owing to the teachings of a North African bishop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:38.160
named Arius who claimed that Jesus was not&nbsp;
co-eternal with God, but was instead a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:44.720
junior celestial that had been created by&nbsp;
God. Throughout his Life, Eusebius tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:51.600
make clear that Constantine aimed for a church&nbsp;
that was unified politically and theologically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:57.600
He stated that Constantine uttered the following&nbsp;
in his address to the assembled bishops: “I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:05.360
that my desires will be most completely fulfilled&nbsp;
when I can see you all united in one judgment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:12.160
and that common spirit of peace and concord&nbsp;
prevailing among you all.” On the disunity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:19.360
the Christian community, Constantine commented&nbsp;
specifically: “Begin from this moment to discard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:25.680
the causes of that disunion which has existed&nbsp;
among you, and remove the perplexities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:33.920
controversy by embracing the principles of peace.”&nbsp;
Yet what Eusebius depicted as an emperor bent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:40.480
establishing peace, could just as easily be&nbsp;
interpreted as a power grab. Constantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:47.440
the dual Pagan-Christian ruler, wanted a&nbsp;
unified church without divisions. Such a Church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:56.480
unified and bound to the state, would be much&nbsp;
easier for him to dominate as Emperor.
&nbsp;

00:30:56.480 --> 00:31:02.240
While the nature of Constantine’s Christianity is&nbsp;
clearly open to debate, there is no denying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:08.880
his actions were incredibly successful. Estimates&nbsp;
are that there were perhaps only around a quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:15.920
of a million Christians throughout the Roman&nbsp;
Empire in 200 AD, out of a total population of as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:23.040
many as 50 or 60 million people. Undoubtedly that&nbsp;
number had increased to a few million Christians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:31.680
by the time Constantine was proclaimed as emperor&nbsp;
in York in 306 AD. But Christians were still very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:38.640
much in the minority across the empire. That all&nbsp;
changed because of Constantine’s reign. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:45.840
not unique in deciding to tolerate Christianity&nbsp;
at the start of the 310s AD. Both Licinius and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:52.080
Galerius had decided on the same course of&nbsp;
action. He was also a peculiar Christian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:58.160
one who was able to integrate worship of the&nbsp;
Christian god into his own polytheism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:05.280
included strong veneration of the Sun deity, Sol&nbsp;
Invictus. However, he laid the groundwork for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:11.600
establishment of a strong Christian church and&nbsp;
his reign was long enough that, by the end of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:18.240
the triumph of Christianity as a state religion&nbsp;
was assured to a large extent. In the decades that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:25.360
followed, millions of people began converting&nbsp;
to Christianity across the empire. 
	
&nbsp;

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:32.720
Constantine’s reign is understandably the focus&nbsp;
for the creation of the Christian church and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:38.880
the building of Constantinople. However, there&nbsp;
were numerous other issues which Constantine was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:45.520
concerned with. The fiscal crisis that had gripped&nbsp;
the empire in the third century had not gone away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:50.480
While the Romans were brilliant engineers and&nbsp;
administrators, they had a limited grasp of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:57.120
economics. The crisis of the third century&nbsp;
had forced them to consider monetary policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:04.960
Diocletian had issued an Edict on Maximum Prices&nbsp;
in 301 AD to try to curb inflation, setting upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:12.400
limits to both prices and wages. Numerous emperors&nbsp;
had tried other methods, engaging in recoinage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.400 --> 00:33:18.480
whereby new coins were issued with higher silver&nbsp;
and gold purity to create fresh confidence in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:26.160
coins that were circulating. Diocletian also tried&nbsp;
this, issuing a new silver coin that was said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:32.240
be pure silver. However, it wasn’t long before&nbsp;
versions of the coin began to circulate where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:38.800
silver was mixed with other metals. Constantine’s&nbsp;
innovation was that that he began issuing ‘billon’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:46.000
coins that were a mix of silver and alloy metals.&nbsp;
He did mint and distribute a new gold coin as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:51.440
kind of premier piece of coinage, but in issuing&nbsp;
the ‘billon’ he was getting rid of the pretence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:57.360
that the silver coins in circulation were pure.&nbsp;
He also ordered the melting down of statues made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:04.400
of gold, silver and bronze from Pagan temples&nbsp;
and used the metal to have new coins minted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:09.440
Christian writers subsequently cheered&nbsp;
on this activity, interpreting it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:15.680
sign of Constantine’s animosity towards&nbsp;
the old Pagan religion. In reality it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:21.520
could be interpreted as a practical measure,&nbsp;
allowing the state to acquire precious metals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:29.040
rather than any indication of his antagonism&nbsp;
towards the old polytheistic religious cults.  
&nbsp;

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:36.480
Constantine’s reign, like that of any Roman&nbsp;
emperor, witnessed the construction of monuments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:43.840
and statues. What marked Constantine’s time&nbsp;
in power as distinct was that his reign lasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:51.280
over three decades, so he simply built more than&nbsp;
others. What is unusual is that, despite creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:58.560
his own capital city at Constantinople, the two&nbsp;
pieces of art and architecture which Constantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:06.640
is most famous for were both constructed in old&nbsp;
Rome in Italy, not the New Rome on the Bosporus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:13.440
These are the Arch of Constantine and the Colossus&nbsp;
of Constantine. The Arch of Constantine lies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:19.280
between the Colosseum and the Palatine Hill in&nbsp;
Rome and is still standing to this day. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.280 --> 00:35:25.040
a classic Roman commemorative arch, celebrating&nbsp;
Constantine’s victory over Maxentius at the Battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:32.320
of the Milvian Bridge. What is remarkable about&nbsp;
the Arch of Constantine is its sheer size. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:40.320
the largest triumphal arch in the city, standing&nbsp;
21 metres tall. By comparison, the Arch of Titus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:47.840
built to commemorate Roman victory in the&nbsp;
First Roman-Jewish War between 66 AD and 73 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:54.960
is 15 metres tall. This being said, Constantine&nbsp;
was magpie-like in the methods he allowed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.960 --> 00:36:00.240
architects to employ. Many of the reliefs&nbsp;
decorating the outside of his arch were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:06.960
literally ripped out of the triumphal arches of&nbsp;
other emperors and recycled for his monument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:12.080
The Colossus of Constantine was erected&nbsp;
in the Basilica of Maxentius in the Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:18.960
Forum. It would have stood 15 metres high&nbsp;
when first built in ancient times. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:25.120
it was destroyed in its original format in ancient&nbsp;
times and was possibly broken up for looting when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:32.480
the Goths sacked the city in 410 AD. The head,&nbsp;
which alone is two and a half metres tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:39.200
has survived, along with some other fragments. A&nbsp;
full replica of the enormous statue was installed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:49.120
in the Capitoline Museum in Rome in 2024. 
Constantine’s later reign was not entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:57.440
devoid of controversy. In the summer of 326 AD, as&nbsp;
work accelerated to establish the New Rome on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.440 --> 00:37:03.360
Bosporus, Constantine appears to have ordered&nbsp;
the killing of both his wife, Fausta, and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:09.920
eldest child, Crispus, the son from his earlier&nbsp;
relationship with Minervina. His motives for doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:17.120
so are not entirely clear. Eusebius conveniently&nbsp;
avoided mentioning the pair and their murders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:22.400
The general consensus amongst later writers&nbsp;
was that Crispus was killed first and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:28.640
Fausta was murdered a few weeks later in a&nbsp;
bathhouse. Zosimus, a historian writing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:34.480
the very end of the fifth century AD stated that&nbsp;
Constantine acted when he learned that the pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:41.120
had become engaged in a sexual relationship with&nbsp;
each other. He stated that Constantine “killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:47.360
Crispus … when he incurred suspicion of him having&nbsp;
sexual relations with his stepmother Fausta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:53.360
without taking any notice of the laws of nature.”&nbsp;
Zosimus expands on the story to account for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.360 --> 00:38:01.360
pair’s death in detail, writing that “Constantine&nbsp;
then tried to remedy the evil with a greater evil:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:07.280
having ordered baths to be heated above the normal&nbsp;
level, he deposited Fausta in them and brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:13.920
her out when she was dead.” An alternate theory is&nbsp;
that Constantine was trying to stamp his authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:20.720
over the imperial family. He had witnessed&nbsp;
Maxentius overpower his own father, Maximian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:26.880
in the western provinces nearly two decades&nbsp;
earlier. As a result, Constantine may have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:32.480
concerned that something similar could happen as&nbsp;
his sons grew up and their mother schemed on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:39.840
behalf. Thus, the murders of Fausta and Crispus&nbsp;
may have been a strategic initiative to indicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:47.520
that Constantine would not tolerate any effort&nbsp;
to dilute his power as he grew older. 
&nbsp;

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:55.120
Constantine certainly did not betray any signs&nbsp;
that he was aging poorly through his fifties and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.120 --> 00:39:00.640
into his sixties. He continued to campaign&nbsp;
militarily throughout the remainder of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:07.680
reign. The empire was more secure in the early&nbsp;
fourth century AD than it had been half a century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:14.640
earlier when the Franks, Alemanni and Sarmatians&nbsp;
had penetrated deep into Gaul and the Balkans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:20.960
However, even at this later point in Constantine’s&nbsp;
lifetime, the borders were still in a precarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:27.680
state. Constantine had been able to secure&nbsp;
the frontier in Germania early on in his reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:32.960
through the location of his capital being&nbsp;
established at Trier, near the River Rhine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:39.360
in the years prior to the Battle of the Milvian&nbsp;
Bridge. During these years, while an unstable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.360 --> 00:39:46.480
peace existed between him, Maxentius and Maximian&nbsp;
in the west, Constantine had campaigned against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:53.760
various Germanic tribes such as the Bructeri, as&nbsp;
well as the Franks and Alemanni. Constantine also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:58.560
left behind reinforced border defences&nbsp;
along the Rhine when he went eastwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:58.560 --> 00:40:06.560
to confront Licinius. 
Thereafter, Constantine’s focus when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:13.360
to Rome’s borders was in campaigning against the&nbsp;
Sarmatians and other groups in the Balkans. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:20.240
tribes had been in control of large parts of Dacia&nbsp;
in modern-day Romania for half a century. This had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:26.560
come after the Emperor Aurelian had strategically&nbsp;
withdrawn from the lands north of the River Danube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:32.880
back when Constantine was an infant. With the&nbsp;
border along the Rhine reinforced, the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:38.880
secure internally, and the Romans enjoying an&nbsp;
extended period of peace with Persia in the east,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:44.720
Constantine developed his ambition to try to&nbsp;
reconquer Dacia. Crispus campaigned against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.720 --> 00:40:52.880
the Alemanni and secured the gap between the Rhine&nbsp;
and Danube Rivers in the mid-320s. Perhaps it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.880 --> 00:40:59.360
this military success which cost Crispus his life&nbsp;
not long afterwards, as his father became wary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.360 --> 00:41:07.360
his ambitions. Thereafter, with the western flank&nbsp;
secure, Constantine campaigned into Dacia. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:16.640
328 AD and 332 AD he was active almost every&nbsp;
year along the frontier in what we now call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:23.920
Serbia and Romania. Here, Constantine won several&nbsp;
victories, establishing a series of new defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:32.560
structures, although his claims that he fully&nbsp;
reconquered Dacia were exaggerated. 
&nbsp;

00:41:32.560 --> 00:41:38.800
Constantine was active until late in his life.&nbsp;
He was preparing a large offensive against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:46.640
Persians in 336 AD and was said to have developed&nbsp;
a plan to be baptised like Jesus in the waters of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:53.680
the River Jordan on his way through the Levant.&nbsp;
However, the plan was never implemented. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.680 --> 00:42:02.640
late winter or early spring of 337 AD, Constantine&nbsp;
became ill. He never fully recovered. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:08.960
he withdrew to Nicomedia, where he had spent so&nbsp;
much of his life in his youth and early adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.960 --> 00:42:17.280
years. There, the man who had begun converting the&nbsp;
Roman Empire to Christianity, was finally baptised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:25.280
on his deathbed. Strangely, the cleric who carried&nbsp;
out the ceremony was Eusebius of Nicomedia, not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:32.400
be confused with his biographer of the same name.&nbsp;
This other Eusebius was an adherent of the Arian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:39.200
heresy that the Council of Nicaea had tried to&nbsp;
eradicate. Not long afterwards, Constantine died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:48.480
on the 22nd of May 337 AD. He was probably around&nbsp;
65 years of age, though we cannot be sure. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:54.960
laid to rest in a sarcophagus in the Church of&nbsp;
the Holy Apostles in Constantinople. His remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.840
were destroyed at some stage in the thirteenth&nbsp;
century during the chaos that had enveloped the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:07.680
city following its conquest by the Christians&nbsp;
of the Fourth Crusade. 
&nbsp;

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:16.240
Constantine was succeeded upon his death by three&nbsp;
of his sons, Constantine II, Constantius II and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:23.680
Constans I. However, while the Constantinian&nbsp;
Dynasty managed to hang on to imperial power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:23.680 --> 00:43:30.160
there was internal conflict. The brothers&nbsp;
quickly became rivals. Constantine II was killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:39.360
while campaigning into Italy. Constantius II and&nbsp;
Constans I then ruled together down to 350 AD when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:46.400
Constans was usurped and killed in the western&nbsp;
provinces by a rebel general named Magnentius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:54.640
Constantius was the longest-lived of these sons&nbsp;
of Constantine the Great. When he died in 361 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.640 --> 00:44:01.840
he was succeeded by his first cousin, Constantine&nbsp;
the Great’s nephew, Julian. This young emperor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:06.880
clearly a man of ability, though he broke&nbsp;
completely with the religious tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:13.600
established by his uncle. In direct contrast to&nbsp;
Constantine’s apparent embrace of Christianity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:20.160
Julian began trying to convert the empire back&nbsp;
to Paganism and re-orientate it towards Hellenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:26.640
philosophy. Julian could well have changed the&nbsp;
history of the ancient world if he had ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:33.600
for three decades like his uncle and been able&nbsp;
pursue his goals for more than 30 years. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:39.920
he died less than two years after he assumed the&nbsp;
imperial purple while campaigning against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:48.000
Persians in the east. His premature passing in the&nbsp;
summer of 363 AD brought the Constantinian Dynasty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:55.440
to an end. Thereafter the empire reverted to a&nbsp;
less linear form of succession. All of Julian’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.440 --> 00:45:01.280
successors were Christians. Many of them, like&nbsp;
the Emperor Theodosius, introduced laws which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:06.720
specifically aimed to persecute non-Christians&nbsp;
in ways that would have been unthinkable in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:13.760
Constantine’s time. The shift towards Christianity&nbsp;
becoming a religion of persecution thus happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:20.480
in the second half of the fourth century,&nbsp;
not during the reign of Constantine. 
&nbsp;

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:28.240
Constantine I was probably the second most&nbsp;
important ruler of the Roman Empire in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.240 --> 00:45:33.520
500 years between the establishment of&nbsp;
the imperial system in 27 BC and the fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:40.640
of the Western Roman Empire in 476 AD. His&nbsp;
importance within the history of the Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:47.040
Empire was eclipsed only by Caesar Augustus, the&nbsp;
man who created the empire and returned the Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:54.080
world to stability after decades of civil war and&nbsp;
anarchy. Constantine’s reign was also the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.080 --> 00:46:01.760
longest in that time period. His ascent was both&nbsp;
unexpected and typical of the time he lived in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:06.240
On the one hand, a stable system of&nbsp;
succession via a college of emperors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:13.200
had been set up by Diocletian when Constantine&nbsp;
was a young man. If this had operated properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:18.320
Constantine would not have become&nbsp;
the ruler of the empire. Conversely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:24.560
if looked at from the perspective of the wider&nbsp;
third century in which Constantine was born, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:31.680
rise to power via civil wars and rivalries with&nbsp;
other would-be emperors was completely normal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:37.680
What was altogether different about Constantine&nbsp;
was what he did when he became emperor. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:43.680
securing control over the western half of the&nbsp;
empire, he issued an official edict of toleration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:48.800
for Christian worship which was followed by&nbsp;
the organisation of the Christian Church into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:58.240
a mass political institution. After becoming the&nbsp;
undisputed ruler of the entire empire in 324 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:05.520
he then built a great new capital in the east. The&nbsp;
conversion of the Roman Empire to Christianity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:11.360
and the construction of what would become one of&nbsp;
the greatest cities of the world throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:21.760
medieval era and down to the present day, are the&nbsp;
two great achievements of Constantine’s reign. 
&nbsp;

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:22.960
 
And yet, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:30.960
is still no certainty today, 1700 years later,&nbsp;
as to what Constantine’s real religious beliefs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:37.280
were. It remains a matter of debate as to whether&nbsp;
the conversion of the empire was facilitated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:43.920
Constantine’s personal beliefs, or whether it came&nbsp;
from his tactical political manoeuvre to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:50.640
imperial stability through greater tolerance of&nbsp;
rival religions. In the end, his motives really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:56.560
do not matter. Constantine stands at the very&nbsp;
centre of a religious and political revolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:56.560 --> 00:48:04.000
in the Late Roman Empire which changed the history&nbsp;
of the world. For that reason, he surely deserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:13.760
to be called Constantine the Great. 
What do you think of Constantine the Great?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.760 --> 00:48:20.080
Was he a genuine Christian believer? Or was he&nbsp;
an opportunist who saw the Christian Church as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:26.160
something which he could utilise to cement&nbsp;
his control over the Roman Empire? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:51.520
let us know in the comment section, and in the&nbsp;
meantime, thank you very much for watching.

